keywords(s_book_14_0056, ['attempting', 'effect', 'replicate', 'study']).
sentence(s_book_14_0057, 'book', 14, 48, 'unmarked', "The later work reported a boundary advantage at a different colour boundary but not at the light-blue/dark-blue boundary the original claim rests on, and found the relevant responses affected by stimulus frequency and presentation order, concluding that the Russian blue categories are less cleanly structured than the earlier result assumed.").
sentence(s_book_14_0058, 'book', 14, 50, 'unmarked', "That has to be stated here rather than in a footnote, because this is the study the argument of this section was built on.").
sentence(s_book_14_0059, 'book', 14, 50, 'unmarked', "The honest position is now narrower than it was: there is evidence that habitual linguistic categories are recruited during perceptual tasks, the colour case is the most-cited demonstration of it, and the colour case has a serious failed replication.").
sentence(s_book_14_0060, 'book', 14, 50, 'unmarked', "The spatial and numerical evidence below does not depend on it.").
sentence(s_book_14_0061, 'book', 14, 52, 'unmarked', "Some languages do not use body-relative terms like left and right for everyday spatial description, using compass-like absolute terms instead — the equivalent of saying there is an insect on one's north arm.").
sentence(s_book_14_0062, 'book', 14, 52, 'unmarked', "Speakers of such languages maintain accurate orientation in conditions where speakers of body-relative languages become lost, and they perform differently on tasks requiring a spatial array to be reproduced after turning around.").
sentence(s_book_14_0063, 'book', 14, 52, 'unmarked', "Habitual use of a reference frame appears to come with habitual tracking of what that frame requires.").
hedged(s_book_14_0063).
sentence(s_book_14_0064, 'book', 14, 54, 'unmarked', "Languages with very restricted counting vocabulary look like the sharpest case, and the detail of what was actually found is more interesting than the headline.").
sentence(s_book_14_0065, 'book', 14, 56, 'unmarked', "The most-discussed example is an Amazonian language two of whose quantity words were initially glossed as one and two, and later reanalysed by the same researcher as meaning something closer to small amount and larger amount — with experimental work supporting the second reading, since speakers used the supposed word for \"one\" of as many as six objects.").
